WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:09.680
So today, we are going to
talk about inference scaling.

00:00:09.680 --> 00:00:13.100
We briefly hinted on this
in the first lecture,

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:17.600
so there are three stages
for LLM kind of development.

00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:19.140
The first one is pre-training.

00:00:19.140 --> 00:00:22.860
This stage is historically
takes a lot more time.

00:00:22.860 --> 00:00:24.780
It could take several months.

00:00:24.780 --> 00:00:30.880
A whole lot of GPUs that
are used for training these.

00:00:30.880 --> 00:00:32.780
And then there's the
fine tuning step.

00:00:32.780 --> 00:00:36.120
Usually, this is a less,
or historically has been

00:00:36.120 --> 00:00:39.020
a less compute-intense process.

00:00:39.020 --> 00:00:41.640
If you are talking
about trillions

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.020
of data, tokens in
the pre-training side,

00:00:45.020 --> 00:00:48.560
on the fine tuning side, this
is orders of magnitude less data

00:00:48.560 --> 00:00:50.100
that we use for fine tuning.

00:00:50.100 --> 00:00:52.160
And then there is
the inference part,

00:00:52.160 --> 00:00:54.600
where we get to use the model.

00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.280
And we are going to
see different ways

00:00:58.280 --> 00:01:00.120
that at inference
time, we can make

00:01:00.120 --> 00:01:05.720
the model be better and become
more useful without changing

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:10.000
the parameters of the model
and without any fine tuning

00:01:10.000 --> 00:01:11.960
of the models.

00:01:11.960 --> 00:01:16.740
So let's first start with the
Large Language Monkeys paper.

00:01:16.740 --> 00:01:20.480
Again, you are familiar with the
infinite monkey theorem by now

00:01:20.480 --> 00:01:24.760
because we discussed
it on Monday.

00:01:24.760 --> 00:01:29.520
But to recap, the idea of this
paper is that we have an LLM

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:31.920
and we have a problem as input.

00:01:31.920 --> 00:01:35.200
Instead of generating the
response for this problem

00:01:35.200 --> 00:01:39.440
only once, we are going to
repeatedly ask the same input

00:01:39.440 --> 00:01:41.520
problem again and again.

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:43.040
This could be a
number of times, it

00:01:43.040 --> 00:01:45.880
could be 10 times,
100 times, and so on,

00:01:45.880 --> 00:01:48.420
and we will see how that
affects the performance.

00:01:48.420 --> 00:01:51.400
And then we are going to have
a verifier that can tell us

00:01:51.400 --> 00:01:55.800
which one of these responses
were the correct response,

00:01:55.800 --> 00:02:01.550
and then that is going to be
the output of this system.

00:02:01.550 --> 00:02:06.130
The reason we like that is that
by doing this repeated sampling,

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.910
we can make models
that are inferior.

00:02:08.910 --> 00:02:16.390
For example, a Llama 3-8b or 70b
that is not as capable as GPT-4o

00:02:16.390 --> 00:02:17.690
with single attempt.

00:02:17.690 --> 00:02:20.070
But by doing this
repeated sampling

00:02:20.070 --> 00:02:25.370
and selecting the correct one
among the generated candidates,

00:02:25.370 --> 00:02:29.350
we can significantly
improve the performance

00:02:29.350 --> 00:02:31.710
of these inferior models
and make them better

00:02:31.710 --> 00:02:35.790
than these larger
and proprietary ones.

00:02:35.790 --> 00:02:38.190
And these are-- here
you can see examples

00:02:38.190 --> 00:02:44.510
across a really hard math coding
and different styles of math

00:02:44.510 --> 00:02:49.150
problem question
answering problems.

00:02:49.150 --> 00:02:52.750
Is this clear to everyone?

00:02:52.750 --> 00:02:56.310
It just seems like the models
and smaller models already

00:02:56.310 --> 00:02:58.690
know the answers to
these hard problems,

00:02:58.690 --> 00:03:01.250
and just by doing this
repeated sampling,

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:06.470
we are eliciting and
surfacing those answers.

00:03:06.470 --> 00:03:13.070
It's just they just don't tell
us that in the first trial.

00:03:13.070 --> 00:03:19.430
Now, this paradigm
of repeated sampling,

00:03:19.430 --> 00:03:21.710
works well across
pretty much any domain

00:03:21.710 --> 00:03:26.350
that we have tried in so far,
including agentic benchmarks

00:03:26.350 --> 00:03:30.830
such as SWE-bench, which mimics
how a software engineer goes

00:03:30.830 --> 00:03:36.310
about editing code and
creating new patches.

00:03:36.310 --> 00:03:38.450
So here, we are
seeing the coverage,

00:03:38.450 --> 00:03:43.510
meaning the fraction
of problems that

00:03:43.510 --> 00:03:46.330
are solved by, at least,
one of the samples.

00:03:46.330 --> 00:03:49.710
And here on the x-axis, we have
the number of samples going from

00:03:49.710 --> 00:03:51.870
1 to 1,000.

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:57.900
And what we are seeing here is
that a models such as DeepSeek

00:03:57.900 --> 00:04:00.260
can--

00:04:00.260 --> 00:04:05.180
I believe this was the
DeepSeek-V3 model can outperform

00:04:05.180 --> 00:04:12.140
models such as Claude 3.5 or
o1 preview after 1,000 samples.

00:04:12.140 --> 00:04:14.620
It can solve more coding
and software engineering

00:04:14.620 --> 00:04:16.360
problems than those.

00:04:16.360 --> 00:04:17.820
And the reason
this is interesting

00:04:17.820 --> 00:04:20.940
is that, we are doing--

00:04:20.940 --> 00:04:23.360
in this case for
coding problems,

00:04:23.360 --> 00:04:26.180
if we have unit
tests that we can

00:04:26.180 --> 00:04:29.940
use to select which one of
these samples is correct, then

00:04:29.940 --> 00:04:32.260
we have an end-to-end
automated way

00:04:32.260 --> 00:04:37.240
to create a more capable
model, in this case,

00:04:37.240 --> 00:04:39.480
out of DeepSeek and
open-source model.

00:04:44.820 --> 00:04:49.940
Now, again, we saw pre-training
scaling laws in the last class,

00:04:49.940 --> 00:04:56.220
and that was, we can predictably
reduce the test laws given

00:04:56.220 --> 00:04:58.360
these equations in this paper.

00:04:58.360 --> 00:05:01.440
And these equations might
change given the setup,

00:05:01.440 --> 00:05:06.260
but there is a predictable
path to increase data, amount

00:05:06.260 --> 00:05:08.500
of compute use, and the
number of parameters

00:05:08.500 --> 00:05:14.820
in the model and use that to
predict, or reduce the test

00:05:14.820 --> 00:05:15.440
laws.

00:05:15.440 --> 00:05:18.140
So this is for pre-training,
but it turns out,

00:05:18.140 --> 00:05:22.380
we can define a similar
scaling laws, but this time

00:05:22.380 --> 00:05:25.500
for inference, or
test time compute,

00:05:25.500 --> 00:05:27.960
and that is something that
we showed in this paper.

00:05:27.960 --> 00:05:30.660
Basically, we're showing
that the relationship

00:05:30.660 --> 00:05:34.060
between coverage and
the number of samples

00:05:34.060 --> 00:05:37.420
that are being drawn in
parallel from the model

00:05:37.420 --> 00:05:39.940
follow an exponential power law.

00:05:39.940 --> 00:05:42.060
So assume this says,
coverage, meaning

00:05:42.060 --> 00:05:46.240
how many problems are solved by,
at least, one of the samples,

00:05:46.240 --> 00:05:48.800
and then k is the
number of samples.

00:05:48.800 --> 00:05:51.340
And a and b are these
kind of coefficients

00:05:51.340 --> 00:05:55.500
that we can find through
the curve-fitting parameters

00:05:55.500 --> 00:06:03.220
that we can find through this
scaling behavior of the model.

00:06:03.220 --> 00:06:09.380
So concretely, as we scaled the
number of samples across a range

00:06:09.380 --> 00:06:13.220
of models from
Llama 3 8B, 7, 8B,

00:06:13.220 --> 00:06:16.900
and models like Gemma or Pythia.

00:06:16.900 --> 00:06:20.120
And this is for various
range of parameter sizes,

00:06:20.120 --> 00:06:23.540
going from 70 million
parameters all the way

00:06:23.540 --> 00:06:25.720
to 70 billion parameters.

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:29.260
We can see that we can
predict this power law

00:06:29.260 --> 00:06:35.720
and have a predicted curve
that for the most part,

00:06:35.720 --> 00:06:41.740
very closely follows the actual
practical coverage that we

00:06:41.740 --> 00:06:44.640
are seeing as we increase
the number of samples.

00:06:44.640 --> 00:06:46.700
So basically, the
way to think about it

00:06:46.700 --> 00:06:53.150
is that we can predict to
achieve a certain coverage,

00:06:53.150 --> 00:06:56.130
how many samples we
are going to need

00:06:56.130 --> 00:07:00.330
and how much resources we
should allocate to achieve that.

00:07:06.850 --> 00:07:10.850
And again, these curves
show that this behavior

00:07:10.850 --> 00:07:15.670
is true for a range of models
and a range of parameter sizes.

00:07:15.670 --> 00:07:19.090
Again, one of the very
interesting properties

00:07:19.090 --> 00:07:21.910
that we saw here is that
for a very small model,

00:07:21.910 --> 00:07:26.510
like a 70 million parameter, we
also see this scaling behavior.

00:07:26.510 --> 00:07:29.950
And, of course, this is true
for much larger models as well,

00:07:29.950 --> 00:07:33.090
and it is true across domains.

00:07:33.090 --> 00:07:41.530
Now, we looked a bit more
into why we see this power law

00:07:41.530 --> 00:07:47.570
behavior in repeated
sampling because it

00:07:47.570 --> 00:07:50.390
might be at first a
little non-intuitive,

00:07:50.390 --> 00:07:58.750
because if the probability of
the pass at one being correct,

00:07:58.750 --> 00:08:02.730
the probability of a correct
response to a question

00:08:02.730 --> 00:08:07.090
is, for example, p,
then we can calculate

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:09.730
the probability
of, at least, one

00:08:09.730 --> 00:08:13.650
of the answers being correct at
k for an individual problems.

00:08:13.650 --> 00:08:19.010
So for a problem i, if we have
the pass at one for problem i,

00:08:19.010 --> 00:08:24.810
we can calculate directly what
is pass at k for problem i

00:08:24.810 --> 00:08:28.730
with this exponential equation.

00:08:28.730 --> 00:08:30.510
I hope this is
clear for everybody,

00:08:30.510 --> 00:08:35.210
but basically, this 1 minus p is
the probability of the first k

00:08:35.210 --> 00:08:37.330
answers not being
correct, and then

00:08:37.330 --> 00:08:40.929
1 minus that is the probability
of, at least, one of these k

00:08:40.929 --> 00:08:45.530
answers being correct, so this
is for an individual problem.

00:08:45.530 --> 00:08:49.260
But what we are seeing that
across a suite of problems,

00:08:49.260 --> 00:08:57.508
we are seeing the power
law, scaling law, meaning

00:08:57.508 --> 00:09:00.720
across these problems,
we are seeing

00:09:00.720 --> 00:09:02.460
the equations are changing.

00:09:02.460 --> 00:09:04.880
So the question that
we wanted to answer

00:09:04.880 --> 00:09:11.640
is what behavior should we see
across the problem examples

00:09:11.640 --> 00:09:20.360
in a data set that justifies
our power law scaling laws?

00:09:20.360 --> 00:09:26.600
And by looking into that
further, what was observed

00:09:26.600 --> 00:09:32.280
is that in order to have the
scaling laws that we observed,

00:09:32.280 --> 00:09:35.160
the sufficient and
necessary condition for it

00:09:35.160 --> 00:09:39.100
is that we have a long
tail of hard problems.

00:09:42.480 --> 00:09:45.560
Basically, from a
mathematical perspective,

00:09:45.560 --> 00:09:48.900
we are going to need that
because this is the power law

00:09:48.900 --> 00:09:53.420
scaling, this is the per problem
exponential scaling laws,

00:09:53.420 --> 00:09:55.540
and this is like this sort.

00:09:55.540 --> 00:10:00.960
We need to have this curve for
the past at one of problems

00:10:00.960 --> 00:10:05.680
in order to justify
this power law scaling.

00:10:05.680 --> 00:10:09.620
And empirically, when we
looked into the problems,

00:10:09.620 --> 00:10:12.420
we saw that this
condition holds.

00:10:12.420 --> 00:10:14.520
And again, what
this condition is

00:10:14.520 --> 00:10:21.640
is that we have a long tail of
really hard problems on the most

00:10:21.640 --> 00:10:23.380
complex side of things.

00:10:23.380 --> 00:10:30.640
For example, let's take a look
at this model and the count

00:10:30.640 --> 00:10:33.440
of problems that are
solved at pass at one

00:10:33.440 --> 00:10:36.380
and add more and more samples.

00:10:36.380 --> 00:10:38.720
It seems like across
all these problems,

00:10:38.720 --> 00:10:41.440
a large portion of
problems are simpler

00:10:41.440 --> 00:10:43.340
and they're solved
in pass at one.

00:10:43.340 --> 00:10:45.480
And as we increase
the complexity

00:10:45.480 --> 00:10:48.560
of the problems, where the
pass at one probability

00:10:48.560 --> 00:10:50.580
is going to become
lower and lower,

00:10:50.580 --> 00:10:56.040
we have a long tail of problems
that have that property.

00:10:56.040 --> 00:10:58.680
So these curves
that you see here

00:10:58.680 --> 00:11:03.240
from high at simpler problems
and low at harder and harder

00:11:03.240 --> 00:11:05.880
problems, meaning lower
and lower paths at one

00:11:05.880 --> 00:11:08.640
is the type of problems,
is the type of behavior

00:11:08.640 --> 00:11:11.700
that we are seeing
across these tasks,

00:11:11.700 --> 00:11:15.520
and that is why we
can see the scaling

00:11:15.520 --> 00:11:20.360
laws that we have observed.

00:11:20.360 --> 00:11:30.200
Now, what this means for the
science and the engineering

00:11:30.200 --> 00:11:34.560
around LLMs is that while
previously companies

00:11:34.560 --> 00:11:37.280
would spend hundreds
of millions of dollars,

00:11:37.280 --> 00:11:42.310
or billions of dollars
on pre-training alone,

00:11:42.310 --> 00:11:44.690
and then a lot
less on fine tuning

00:11:44.690 --> 00:11:48.110
and then almost like
nothing at each inference

00:11:48.110 --> 00:11:50.470
call because it used
to be like a one time

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:53.910
back and forth between the user
and the query and the model.

00:11:53.910 --> 00:11:56.150
Now we have a new
paradigm, where

00:11:56.150 --> 00:12:00.210
we can spend a whole lot
more compute on inference,

00:12:00.210 --> 00:12:03.290
and use that to increase
the capability of model.

00:12:03.290 --> 00:12:06.490
And this compute the inference
compute can be done offline.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:10.550
We can release our agents
to go solve a problem

00:12:10.550 --> 00:12:13.990
and just keep generating
tokens and keep

00:12:13.990 --> 00:12:17.970
improving the quality of the
answers that they generate.

00:12:20.510 --> 00:12:24.550
Now, when we talk about
this repeated sampling,

00:12:24.550 --> 00:12:29.710
something that's
important to have in mind

00:12:29.710 --> 00:12:32.750
is the need for
automated verification

00:12:32.750 --> 00:12:35.090
because if you have
a bunch of samples,

00:12:35.090 --> 00:12:38.470
we still need to know which one
of them are the correct ones

00:12:38.470 --> 00:12:40.810
and how to go about
selecting that.

00:12:43.430 --> 00:12:48.470
In certain domains, verification
is easier than the others.

00:12:48.470 --> 00:12:52.830
For example, for certain
types of math problems,

00:12:52.830 --> 00:12:55.550
you can use formal proofs.

00:12:55.550 --> 00:12:59.230
So given a proof,
the strategy, we

00:12:59.230 --> 00:13:03.110
can run them by these
formal proof software tools

00:13:03.110 --> 00:13:06.750
to verify whether each
step is correct or not.

00:13:06.750 --> 00:13:09.250
We can write unit
tests for coding.

00:13:09.250 --> 00:13:15.130
And in some cases, writing a
unit test arguably, arguably,

00:13:15.130 --> 00:13:18.990
is a much simpler task than
writing the entire program that

00:13:18.990 --> 00:13:21.950
solves a problem,
so the unit test

00:13:21.950 --> 00:13:24.870
can be written, for
example, by humans,

00:13:24.870 --> 00:13:29.990
and we can use that
as our verifier.

00:13:29.990 --> 00:13:33.210
There are other cases
like AI as a compiler,

00:13:33.210 --> 00:13:37.550
and this is one of the projects
in the lab and the direction

00:13:37.550 --> 00:13:39.740
that I'm very excited
about, and that

00:13:39.740 --> 00:13:42.700
is when we can generate
compiler lower level

00:13:42.700 --> 00:13:48.140
code, like CUDA for a source
code like PyTorch with LLM.

00:13:48.140 --> 00:13:50.100
And the reason
this is verifiable

00:13:50.100 --> 00:13:56.860
is that, for example
here, we can always

00:13:56.860 --> 00:14:01.020
compare the output of CUDA
with the source PyTorch

00:14:01.020 --> 00:14:04.320
and see if they're the
same for any given input.

00:14:04.320 --> 00:14:08.780
So we don't need if we want
to say whether the model has

00:14:08.780 --> 00:14:13.160
generated the correct CUDA code
for our source PyTorch code,

00:14:13.160 --> 00:14:19.120
we can just see whether their
outputs match for any inputs.

00:14:21.980 --> 00:14:24.440
For those of you who
are not familiar CUDA,

00:14:24.440 --> 00:14:28.180
I hope you all are, but
it's a lower level language

00:14:28.180 --> 00:14:31.820
for writing more hardware
aware and optimized

00:14:31.820 --> 00:14:35.840
for hardware code on GPUs.

00:14:38.620 --> 00:14:43.500
For example, here,
we have a data set,

00:14:43.500 --> 00:14:47.180
or benchmark on CUDA
generation called KernelBench.

00:14:47.180 --> 00:14:52.380
And here, again, we are seeing
the same linear improving

00:14:52.380 --> 00:14:56.440
coverage in this case
for CUDA code generation

00:14:56.440 --> 00:14:58.460
as we increase the
number of samples

00:14:58.460 --> 00:15:00.160
that we take from the model.

00:15:00.160 --> 00:15:02.220
And in this case,
we have this, again,

00:15:02.220 --> 00:15:05.260
by default, perfect
verifier that we

00:15:05.260 --> 00:15:10.740
can use for measuring the
correctness of our CUDA

00:15:10.740 --> 00:15:13.300
generated code.

00:15:13.300 --> 00:15:15.580
And there are other
examples of this.

00:15:18.100 --> 00:15:20.140
Basically, any
kind of translation

00:15:20.140 --> 00:15:21.800
between two languages.

00:15:21.800 --> 00:15:27.000
If you want to port from Python,
or C++ to Java, or vice versa,

00:15:27.000 --> 00:15:28.760
again, this is true.

00:15:28.760 --> 00:15:34.420
You can measure the
equivalency much easier.

00:15:34.420 --> 00:15:38.060
Now there are domains that
we don't have verifier for.

00:15:38.060 --> 00:15:40.700
And in those domains,
what we observe

00:15:40.700 --> 00:15:43.900
is that there is a
large gap between best

00:15:43.900 --> 00:15:46.980
of n methods such
as majority vote

00:15:46.980 --> 00:15:52.220
voting and model-based
rankers and what is

00:15:52.220 --> 00:15:54.640
the true coverage of the model.

00:15:54.640 --> 00:15:58.700
Let's take a look
at one example here.

00:15:58.700 --> 00:16:01.240
So here, we compared a
few different methods.

00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:03.460
We are going to learn
about reward models

00:16:03.460 --> 00:16:06.820
or verifiers on Monday
more, but let's take

00:16:06.820 --> 00:16:08.360
a look at these examples.

00:16:08.360 --> 00:16:11.300
So we have a number of
samples, and then we

00:16:11.300 --> 00:16:13.480
are measuring the
success rate here,

00:16:13.480 --> 00:16:17.900
like basically what would be
the output of this system?

00:16:17.900 --> 00:16:20.060
Majority voting,
or the green one

00:16:20.060 --> 00:16:24.740
is we just see which answer
among the generated answers

00:16:24.740 --> 00:16:28.760
has appeared the most,
so based on that,

00:16:28.760 --> 00:16:31.180
we just select that
answer or that response

00:16:31.180 --> 00:16:33.340
as the output of our system.

00:16:33.340 --> 00:16:34.930
Does that make sense?

00:16:34.930 --> 00:16:36.570
It's just majority voting.

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:39.590
And the blue line
here is coverage.

00:16:39.590 --> 00:16:43.210
This is assuming we had a
perfect selector, or verifier

00:16:43.210 --> 00:16:44.210
in this case.

00:16:44.210 --> 00:16:47.090
And as you can see here,
there is a large difference

00:16:47.090 --> 00:16:53.530
between majority voting, which
plateaus after 10 or 50 samples,

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:55.970
and what we could
possibly get if we

00:16:55.970 --> 00:17:01.050
had a perfect verifier, so
this is a very, very large gap.

00:17:01.050 --> 00:17:05.170
Especially, and this gap
is even more pronounced

00:17:05.170 --> 00:17:06.630
if you have harder problems.

00:17:06.630 --> 00:17:09.849
So for example, the
capital MATH here, data set

00:17:09.849 --> 00:17:13.490
is a harder, more complex
data set than GSM8K

00:17:13.490 --> 00:17:16.050
And here, we compared
other methods

00:17:16.050 --> 00:17:25.089
like using a reward model
that measures, gives

00:17:25.089 --> 00:17:30.290
a reward per answer, and we
take the best of those, or ways

00:17:30.290 --> 00:17:31.590
to combine these tools.

00:17:31.590 --> 00:17:34.310
We will learn more about
these reward models.

00:17:34.310 --> 00:17:37.690
Basically, they're
LLM-based LLMs

00:17:37.690 --> 00:17:44.290
that are trained to score
the response quality.

00:17:44.290 --> 00:17:47.850
And even with those,
there is a large gap.

00:17:47.850 --> 00:17:52.770
And this is what we call the
generation verification gap.

00:17:52.770 --> 00:17:54.490
So the generation,
it turns out, we

00:17:54.490 --> 00:17:57.470
can generate a lot
of good responses,

00:17:57.470 --> 00:18:01.110
but the verification
is basically this gap.

00:18:01.110 --> 00:18:02.890
This tells us how
much we can actually

00:18:02.890 --> 00:18:09.290
capture those responses
or those correct answers.

00:18:09.290 --> 00:18:13.410
The reason methods such as
majority voting doesn't work is

00:18:13.410 --> 00:18:17.630
that, as we look into
the quality of answers,

00:18:17.630 --> 00:18:21.770
it turns out that for some
problems that are hardest

00:18:21.770 --> 00:18:27.010
to solve, sometimes we see that
they're solved maybe even once,

00:18:27.010 --> 00:18:32.120
or two times, or three times
across the 1,000 or 10,000

00:18:32.120 --> 00:18:34.600
samples that we
take from the model.

00:18:34.600 --> 00:18:40.840
So these correct problems are
actually very rarely solved

00:18:40.840 --> 00:18:43.940
by the model with the samples
that we are taking from it,

00:18:43.940 --> 00:18:48.320
so it makes sense that a
majority voting mechanism cannot

00:18:48.320 --> 00:18:51.240
capture those
because they're rare.

00:18:51.240 --> 00:18:53.820
But for many of the
problems in this case,

00:18:53.820 --> 00:18:56.200
especially for the
GSM8K, which is simpler

00:18:56.200 --> 00:19:00.600
because the answers, or
responses are simpler

00:19:00.600 --> 00:19:04.660
to generate, majority voting
works for many of the problems,

00:19:04.660 --> 00:19:07.800
but even in this case,
for some of the hardest

00:19:07.800 --> 00:19:12.240
problems, the frequency of
correct answers actually

00:19:12.240 --> 00:19:14.080
pretty low.

00:19:14.080 --> 00:19:16.700
So that makes it harder from
a verification perspective

00:19:16.700 --> 00:19:18.300
how do you find them?

