00:26:55.410 --> 00:26:58.890
So with the monkeys
framework, we

00:26:58.890 --> 00:27:01.310
saw that repeated
sampling is effective,

00:27:01.310 --> 00:27:04.550
but there are other
ways to scale sampling.

00:27:04.550 --> 00:27:06.770
And let's take a
look at this paper.

00:27:06.770 --> 00:27:09.930
Scaling LLM test time
compute optimally

00:27:09.930 --> 00:27:12.910
can be more effective than
scaling model parameters.

00:27:12.910 --> 00:27:15.930
We going to touch on
certain aspects of it that

00:27:15.930 --> 00:27:20.410
is most relevant to test time
scaling, so there are two ways.

00:27:20.410 --> 00:27:22.770
Parallel sampling
is one way for us

00:27:22.770 --> 00:27:27.850
to generate multiple
answers per input question.

00:27:27.850 --> 00:27:31.450
But there is another way to
generate, or generate answers,

00:27:31.450 --> 00:27:34.490
or use test time scaling
to improve the quality,

00:27:34.490 --> 00:27:38.390
and that is when we do
sequential revisions.

00:27:38.390 --> 00:27:42.330
For example, the model comes
up with one initial steps

00:27:42.330 --> 00:27:44.650
of the problem, or
one initial approach

00:27:44.650 --> 00:27:48.770
for solving the problem,
and it keeps revising that,

00:27:48.770 --> 00:27:52.310
or it keeps improving
and adding to that.

00:27:52.310 --> 00:27:55.210
And that is the sequential
approach, so instead

00:27:55.210 --> 00:27:57.030
of asking the model
multiple times,

00:27:57.030 --> 00:28:01.870
we let the model know that it
can keep revising its answers,

00:28:01.870 --> 00:28:03.890
look into that from
a different angles,

00:28:03.890 --> 00:28:08.530
and continue doing so
until it's confident

00:28:08.530 --> 00:28:10.650
that it's ready to
generate an answer

00:28:10.650 --> 00:28:12.290
and then finally output that.

00:28:12.290 --> 00:28:12.970
Yes.

00:28:12.970 --> 00:28:17.880
So is this by our own
prompting or is it the models?

00:28:17.880 --> 00:28:20.940
In this case, let's assume
by our own prompting,

00:28:20.940 --> 00:28:23.220
but when you look at
the reasoning models,

00:28:23.220 --> 00:28:24.560
they are now trained.

00:28:24.560 --> 00:28:27.760
Internally, we see this
kind of behavior in them,

00:28:27.760 --> 00:28:35.360
so they do this revisions
sometimes in their own answers.

00:28:35.360 --> 00:28:39.860
Now, there is another way
to scale test time compute,

00:28:39.860 --> 00:28:43.820
and that is how we
select the answers.

00:28:43.820 --> 00:28:47.960
So for the parallel sampling,
we looked into the outcome

00:28:47.960 --> 00:28:50.420
like there are n
different responses.

00:28:50.420 --> 00:28:53.700
We look into the outcome and
see if they're correct or not.

00:28:53.700 --> 00:28:56.540
We mentioned unit
tests and so on,

00:28:56.540 --> 00:28:59.120
but there's also this other
way of learning based,

00:28:59.120 --> 00:29:03.400
or outcome reward models, where
they look into the outcome

00:29:03.400 --> 00:29:06.040
and say whether
they're correct or not.

00:29:06.040 --> 00:29:08.480
Of course, their
accuracy, or quality

00:29:08.480 --> 00:29:12.860
can be limited, especially
if we go to new domains.

00:29:12.860 --> 00:29:16.000
But outcome-based reward
model is another way

00:29:16.000 --> 00:29:17.880
that they look into
the final answer

00:29:17.880 --> 00:29:19.220
and they generate a score.

00:29:19.220 --> 00:29:23.880
Basically, these models are
trained to give a reward based

00:29:23.880 --> 00:29:25.340
on an output.

00:29:25.340 --> 00:29:28.120
Then there is also the
process reward models.

00:29:28.120 --> 00:29:31.200
So the difference between
process and outcome

00:29:31.200 --> 00:29:33.360
is that process
reward models are

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:38.940
designed to give a score per
step of the generated solution.

00:29:38.940 --> 00:29:40.780
For example, we have
a math problems,

00:29:40.780 --> 00:29:43.560
there are five steps
that the model takes

00:29:43.560 --> 00:29:48.200
before generating a solution.

00:29:48.200 --> 00:29:51.560
Process reward models, again, we
learn about this more on Monday,

00:29:51.560 --> 00:29:56.000
I believe, are trained to
give a score for the steps

00:29:56.000 --> 00:29:58.960
of the generation.

00:29:58.960 --> 00:30:02.520
So that's the difference
between the two.

00:30:02.520 --> 00:30:05.380
Now, for best of n
sampling with a verifier,

00:30:05.380 --> 00:30:06.620
again, the problem is easy.

00:30:06.620 --> 00:30:09.080
We generate parallel
samples, and then we

00:30:09.080 --> 00:30:12.120
can use an outcome-based
reward model

00:30:12.120 --> 00:30:15.480
to see which one has
the highest score

00:30:15.480 --> 00:30:20.800
and then take that
as our answer.

00:30:20.800 --> 00:30:26.240
Then there are other ways that
we can bring these reward models

00:30:26.240 --> 00:30:30.940
into the processing, into our
test time scaling framework.

00:30:30.940 --> 00:30:35.560
For example, here, let's assume
at each level of answering

00:30:35.560 --> 00:30:40.000
this question, we have a
budget of four samples.

00:30:40.000 --> 00:30:43.080
We take these four
samples, and then here,

00:30:43.080 --> 00:30:49.240
use a Process Reward Model
or PRM to in this case, we

00:30:49.240 --> 00:30:53.380
select the top two with the
highest score from our PRM.

00:30:53.380 --> 00:31:00.440
Then again, we sample
from them and we continue.

00:31:00.440 --> 00:31:08.080
And we can have a threshold
for selecting the top two

00:31:08.080 --> 00:31:11.550
kind of responses in
this case and expanding

00:31:11.550 --> 00:31:12.570
the tree from there.

00:31:12.570 --> 00:31:16.030
So here, we are doing
a beam search approach

00:31:16.030 --> 00:31:19.030
that uses the process
reward model to guide

00:31:19.030 --> 00:31:21.670
how we are sampling our model.

00:31:21.670 --> 00:31:24.390
Is that clear?

00:31:24.390 --> 00:31:25.070
Yes.

00:31:25.070 --> 00:31:27.350
How do you choose the options?

00:31:27.350 --> 00:31:28.850
Based on the PRM score.

00:31:28.850 --> 00:31:33.230
So the process reward model
is trained to generate

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:36.150
a score imagine
between 0 and 1 based

00:31:36.150 --> 00:31:41.730
on the step given the question,
and/or just the step alone.

00:31:44.600 --> 00:31:47.230
Is the PRM fine tuned for that?

00:31:47.230 --> 00:31:51.570
Yes, PRMs are generally also
fine tuned from language model.

00:31:51.570 --> 00:31:53.950
As you can imagine,
they would work better

00:31:53.950 --> 00:32:01.830
for in-domain types of tasks, so
if you have a certain benchmark

00:32:01.830 --> 00:32:04.030
that you want to optimize
for, of course, training

00:32:04.030 --> 00:32:07.950
a PRM on a subset of
that task helps you

00:32:07.950 --> 00:32:09.830
with test time
scaling on that task

00:32:09.830 --> 00:32:12.030
better than across new task.

00:32:12.030 --> 00:32:14.330
But because they're
language model based,

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:17.510
they do exhibit some
generalization across tasks

00:32:17.510 --> 00:32:19.830
as well.

00:32:19.830 --> 00:32:21.270
Yes.

00:32:21.270 --> 00:32:24.910
For the process reward,
is this a reward

00:32:24.910 --> 00:32:27.210
that's being
computed in practice?

00:32:27.210 --> 00:32:31.970
I don't know what the research
is per token that is being done,

00:32:31.970 --> 00:32:34.810
or maybe blocks of tokens
and then it's being computed.

00:32:34.810 --> 00:32:35.310
Yes.

00:32:35.310 --> 00:32:36.490
It's not per token.

00:32:36.490 --> 00:32:39.110
It's per step.

00:32:39.110 --> 00:32:41.110
And this is something
we're going to learn about

00:32:41.110 --> 00:32:42.210
in future lectures.

00:32:42.210 --> 00:32:44.530
Imagine, these are
like a math problem,

00:32:44.530 --> 00:32:48.110
and then there are
steps to answer that.

00:32:48.110 --> 00:32:50.630
One way to do it is to
have humans annotate

00:32:50.630 --> 00:32:54.290
the quality of each step like
yes or no, this is good or bad,

00:32:54.290 --> 00:32:57.230
and then training the model
based on each step, which

00:32:57.230 --> 00:33:02.070
could be like you can assume
it's like one intellectually

00:33:02.070 --> 00:33:05.910
meaningful chunk of the
result, or it could be you

00:33:05.910 --> 00:33:08.320
can define it as a
sentence and so on.

00:33:13.940 --> 00:33:19.480
And, of course, as
it's very intuitive,

00:33:19.480 --> 00:33:23.260
if you combine the two of
outcome based reward model

00:33:23.260 --> 00:33:26.160
and parallel based upon
sequential revisions,

00:33:26.160 --> 00:33:27.520
you get better results.

00:33:27.520 --> 00:33:29.300
This field is
still there's a lot

00:33:29.300 --> 00:33:36.240
of unknown questions around
this, but combining the two.

00:33:36.240 --> 00:33:39.140
For example, one simple
way to combine that

00:33:39.140 --> 00:33:46.740
is instead of one sample
and revisions, you do two

00:33:46.740 --> 00:33:49.200
and you get to
two final answers,

00:33:49.200 --> 00:33:55.620
and then you use the best one,
either using your PRM or ORM

00:33:55.620 --> 00:33:57.800
to select how the two are.

00:33:57.800 --> 00:34:00.100
But you're augmenting
each parallel sample

00:34:00.100 --> 00:34:02.380
with these sequential
revisions, and you

00:34:02.380 --> 00:34:05.860
can use a PRM to
guide that search

00:34:05.860 --> 00:34:08.380
for each sequential revision.

00:34:08.380 --> 00:34:12.739
Something that's interesting
is that the PRM, there

00:34:12.739 --> 00:34:16.480
are many off the shelf PRM's
that you can use for your tasks.

00:34:16.480 --> 00:34:18.980
So you can go and say,
take a pre-trained PRM

00:34:18.980 --> 00:34:22.080
and use that to guide the
search for your own task.

00:34:22.080 --> 00:34:24.260
And you can potentially
find a PRM that

00:34:24.260 --> 00:34:28.560
is most relevant to the type
of task that you're doing,

00:34:28.560 --> 00:34:30.800
and you want to do
test time scaling on,

00:34:30.800 --> 00:34:34.060
or you can train
your own PRM and ORM

00:34:34.060 --> 00:34:36.719
based on your own data as well.

00:34:40.139 --> 00:34:45.620
So this paper looked into the
math data set and something like

00:34:45.620 --> 00:34:51.639
12k train set 500 test
questions, used the palm model.

00:34:51.639 --> 00:34:54.340
And what was interesting
here is that they defined

00:34:54.340 --> 00:35:00.140
a notion of difficulty
for measuring

00:35:00.140 --> 00:35:02.560
these different
scaling behaviors,

00:35:02.560 --> 00:35:04.460
and for that, they
looked into the pass

00:35:04.460 --> 00:35:08.820
at one performance of the
model on each problem.

00:35:08.820 --> 00:35:11.900
And by just measuring
that, they define OK, these

00:35:11.900 --> 00:35:13.660
are the problems
that are hard, these

00:35:13.660 --> 00:35:17.020
are the problems based on
how many of the generated

00:35:17.020 --> 00:35:20.580
answers are correct across a
body of a number of samples.

00:35:20.580 --> 00:35:26.320
They had five different bins for
the complexity of the problem,

00:35:26.320 --> 00:35:30.540
and they did that, as we see to
get some conclusion about what

00:35:30.540 --> 00:35:36.380
test time scaling works better
for harder or easier task.

00:35:36.380 --> 00:35:39.660
They also train their
own process reward model,

00:35:39.660 --> 00:35:43.680
and even they fine tune a model
for creating these revisions.

00:35:43.680 --> 00:35:48.200
But right now, every model that
we use pretty much it's capable.

00:35:48.200 --> 00:35:50.520
It's just as long as
it's instruction-tuned,

00:35:50.520 --> 00:35:57.860
is capable of doing revisions,
if you ask the model to do so.

00:35:57.860 --> 00:36:00.980
And there are some
interesting observations here.

00:36:00.980 --> 00:36:04.170
For example, here is
the majority voting

00:36:04.170 --> 00:36:05.650
for these problems.

00:36:05.650 --> 00:36:08.010
The accuracy as the
generation budget

00:36:08.010 --> 00:36:14.290
increases, and then the
purple one is the ORM one.

00:36:14.290 --> 00:36:21.230
And the green one is the PRM
one, and then the blue one,

00:36:21.230 --> 00:36:25.210
if you really try to optimize
different ways of mixing

00:36:25.210 --> 00:36:27.530
and matching things.

00:36:27.530 --> 00:36:31.260
And this area is the one that
still is an open research

00:36:31.260 --> 00:36:33.310
like how do we do that?

00:36:33.310 --> 00:36:36.610
How do we go about mixing
and matching revisions

00:36:36.610 --> 00:36:41.087
and parallel scaling to
optimize the test time

00:36:41.087 --> 00:36:42.670
scaling because at
the end of the day,

00:36:42.670 --> 00:36:46.890
we want for each accuracy, we
want the minimum generation

00:36:46.890 --> 00:36:49.650
budget to hit that accuracy?

00:36:49.650 --> 00:36:53.050
And in a few slides ahead,
we talk about one method

00:36:53.050 --> 00:37:00.370
to optimize this scaling and
budget performance per budget.

00:37:00.370 --> 00:37:03.390
They also looked into these
different bins of difficulty,

00:37:03.390 --> 00:37:06.770
so number 5 is the most
difficult questions

00:37:06.770 --> 00:37:09.670
because the accuracy is
the lowest and number 1.

00:37:09.670 --> 00:37:11.570
And then here on
the right, there's

00:37:11.570 --> 00:37:16.050
these colors is the
sequential to parallel ratio.

00:37:16.050 --> 00:37:20.970
And while they're saying that
OK for the easier problems,

00:37:20.970 --> 00:37:26.530
it seems like this darkest the
darkest purple color achieves

00:37:26.530 --> 00:37:29.170
highest accuracy,
and that's where

00:37:29.170 --> 00:37:34.490
we have a lot more sequential
tokens compared to parallel.

00:37:34.490 --> 00:37:37.510
When the problems are more
difficult, and to some extent,

00:37:37.510 --> 00:37:40.050
those are some of the
more interesting problems,

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:42.550
this optimality
is harder to say.

00:37:42.550 --> 00:37:47.690
It's harder to say whether it's
sequential or the ratio changes

00:37:47.690 --> 00:37:51.650
even from bucket four
to bucket five on how

00:37:51.650 --> 00:37:53.190
you should allocate that.

00:37:57.250 --> 00:38:02.200
Another observation from
this paper was that--

00:38:02.200 --> 00:38:04.320
and this is still
a work in progress,

00:38:04.320 --> 00:38:08.280
and I believe still the
true observation as of today

00:38:08.280 --> 00:38:11.900
is that for easy and
medium questions,

00:38:11.900 --> 00:38:14.480
it seems like
additional test time

00:38:14.480 --> 00:38:18.080
compute can be more
favorable than scaling

00:38:18.080 --> 00:38:21.600
pre-training of the model.

00:38:21.600 --> 00:38:24.000
So here, we have these two.

00:38:24.000 --> 00:38:27.800
The easy are the
green, medium is blue,

00:38:27.800 --> 00:38:31.020
and orange is the
hard questions.

00:38:31.020 --> 00:38:34.400
It seems if we look into the
ratio of inference tokens

00:38:34.400 --> 00:38:46.400
to pre-training tokens, it is
positive for the easy and medium

00:38:46.400 --> 00:38:48.780
problem, but for
the hardest problem,

00:38:48.780 --> 00:38:52.800
it seems larger models
like a more pre-training,

00:38:52.800 --> 00:38:56.200
or larger models
still do better if you

00:38:56.200 --> 00:39:01.000
are from a tokens
optimized perspective.

00:39:01.000 --> 00:39:03.560
And this is something
that we have

00:39:03.560 --> 00:39:07.480
seen in many of our research
in the lab or around the lab

00:39:07.480 --> 00:39:09.320
as well.

00:39:09.320 --> 00:39:14.760
It seems like models, simpler
open source models, or smaller

00:39:14.760 --> 00:39:17.520
models are becoming
more and more useful

00:39:17.520 --> 00:39:20.640
as we take more test time
compute, apply more test

00:39:20.640 --> 00:39:22.160
time compute on them.

00:39:22.160 --> 00:39:24.780
But still, for the very,
very hard problems,

00:39:24.780 --> 00:39:27.520
still the frontier
models, which presumably

00:39:27.520 --> 00:39:30.240
use more pre-training
and they are larger,

00:39:30.240 --> 00:39:35.160
they do better even if we had a
whole lot like infinite budget

00:39:35.160 --> 00:39:36.672
for test time scaling.

00:39:36.672 --> 00:39:38.380
Of course, we don't
have infinite budget,

00:39:38.380 --> 00:39:41.480
but any reasonably
large test time scale.

00:39:41.480 --> 00:39:42.826
Yes.

00:39:42.826 --> 00:39:46.460
In this case, how do you
inference for this pre-training?

00:39:46.460 --> 00:39:48.480
Because it seems
like for inference,

00:39:48.480 --> 00:39:50.720
you use it much
like [INAUDIBLE],

00:39:50.720 --> 00:39:53.558
whereas for pre-training
tokens, it's you use it once

00:39:53.558 --> 00:39:54.600
and you can use it twice.

00:39:54.600 --> 00:39:56.860
Yes, that is true.

00:39:56.860 --> 00:40:00.480
But in general, from
a-- but this is still--

00:40:00.480 --> 00:40:04.700
so basically, yes,
pre-training is done once,

00:40:04.700 --> 00:40:08.900
but scaling is every
time we run the model.

00:40:17.630 --> 00:40:20.160
But still, this is an
interesting observation

00:40:20.160 --> 00:40:24.360
from the perspective of, are
we done doing pre-training

00:40:24.360 --> 00:40:27.880
because we can just keep
sampling from the models we

00:40:27.880 --> 00:40:31.560
have, or no, pre-training
is still helpful

00:40:31.560 --> 00:40:34.560
and we should do more and more?

00:40:34.560 --> 00:40:38.800
Not everyone can
pre-train a large model.

00:40:38.800 --> 00:40:44.120
So even if
pre-training was better

00:40:44.120 --> 00:40:46.100
than test time
scaling in any scale,

00:40:46.100 --> 00:40:49.320
not everyone can do that because
it's a very expensive process.

00:40:49.320 --> 00:40:52.760
So this is very interesting
that for many problems,

00:40:52.760 --> 00:40:54.960
we can do test
time scaling, even

00:40:54.960 --> 00:40:58.910
if we do it every time for a
new problem, because that's

00:40:58.910 --> 00:40:59.810
feasible.

00:40:59.810 --> 00:41:02.490
But also, on the other hand,
for the hardest problems,

00:41:02.490 --> 00:41:04.210
we still need to
do pre-training,

00:41:04.210 --> 00:41:07.110
and better models
still do better.

00:41:07.110 --> 00:41:07.970
That makes sense.

00:41:07.970 --> 00:41:10.990
I was just curious
precisely in the graph

00:41:10.990 --> 00:41:15.250
when it says like the ratio is 1
for retraining of, for instance,

00:41:15.250 --> 00:41:15.890
retraining.

00:41:15.890 --> 00:41:19.110
Does that mean that it's
[INAUDIBLE] training,

00:41:19.110 --> 00:41:23.030
there was also one training
used for a single task?

00:41:23.030 --> 00:41:23.530
No.

00:41:23.530 --> 00:41:25.490
No, it's not exactly that.

00:41:25.490 --> 00:41:27.950
It's more like some
ratio of pre-training

00:41:27.950 --> 00:41:29.530
to inference compute.

00:41:29.530 --> 00:41:34.910
It's not a one-to-one because it
doesn't make sense in that case.

00:41:34.910 --> 00:41:37.950
My question is, why is it
that those larger models still

00:41:37.950 --> 00:41:40.270
count better?

00:41:40.270 --> 00:41:43.710
Can we take a smaller model
and yet make it an expert

00:41:43.710 --> 00:41:45.510
on hot questions?

00:41:45.510 --> 00:41:50.030
You can always fine tune
a model to become better

00:41:50.030 --> 00:41:53.590
on a certain set of
questions, but we

00:41:53.590 --> 00:41:57.390
are talking about a more general
perspective of let's have

00:41:57.390 --> 00:41:59.870
a whole lot of data
that's general, not

00:41:59.870 --> 00:42:08.510
specific to a specific task and
just compare the common recipes

00:42:08.510 --> 00:42:10.530
that we use for training models.

00:42:10.530 --> 00:42:13.190
Let's just follow that
and then see how much

00:42:13.190 --> 00:42:14.930
they do better at test time.

00:42:22.430 --> 00:42:25.470
Any other questions?

00:42:25.470 --> 00:42:30.910
So maybe we discussed some
of these already, but here

00:42:30.910 --> 00:42:35.130
are some more questions
that I give you another two

00:42:35.130 --> 00:42:36.930
minutes, let me check the time.

00:42:36.930 --> 00:42:41.630
Yes, we do have time,
to think about this.

00:42:41.630 --> 00:42:45.750
This helps you absorb
some of the topics

00:42:45.750 --> 00:42:50.730
that we learned, or heard
about in the previous slides,

00:42:50.730 --> 00:42:53.600
and then we discuss
them in two minutes.

00:42:56.340 --> 00:42:59.340
OK, should we start?

00:42:59.340 --> 00:43:01.500
Anyone wants to add
any comments here,

00:43:01.500 --> 00:43:06.020
or any questions you might have?

00:43:06.020 --> 00:43:07.340
Yes.

00:43:07.340 --> 00:43:12.660
So do you try sequential and try
too-- what about tree search?

00:43:12.660 --> 00:43:16.220
And in that case,
you're doing the--

00:43:16.220 --> 00:43:17.640
You're mixing the both.

00:43:17.640 --> 00:43:19.360
Yeah, you're kind
of mixing, right?

00:43:19.360 --> 00:43:19.860
Yeah.

00:43:19.860 --> 00:43:22.000
There is no universal
answer that can be there.

00:43:22.000 --> 00:43:26.100
But the general sentiment
was that does better,

00:43:26.100 --> 00:43:29.540
like some sort of combining
the two than doing--

00:43:29.540 --> 00:43:33.180
[INAUDIBLE] you do, so reduce
a lot of the compute that

00:43:33.180 --> 00:43:35.980
you're doing or reuse
[INAUDIBLE] that you're doing

00:43:35.980 --> 00:43:37.300
because it's a tree--

00:43:37.300 --> 00:43:39.620
Yes, yes.

00:43:39.620 --> 00:43:43.300
That is when we discussed the
beam search stuff and the PRM,

00:43:43.300 --> 00:43:49.620
like bringing a PRM to
cut the tree at each level

00:43:49.620 --> 00:43:53.920
and explore only the
more promising branches.

00:43:57.620 --> 00:43:58.780
Anything else?

00:43:58.780 --> 00:43:59.420
Yes.

00:43:59.420 --> 00:44:06.560
[INAUDIBLE] as the thinking
that that's the easier problem,

00:44:06.560 --> 00:44:08.860
the more perhaps you
have towards the success

00:44:08.860 --> 00:44:09.800
of solving it.

00:44:09.800 --> 00:44:13.380
Therefore, it could be
more sequential [INAUDIBLE]

00:44:13.380 --> 00:44:16.100
problem because you would--
there are few students solving,

00:44:16.100 --> 00:44:18.980
so you would need
many parallel process

00:44:18.980 --> 00:44:22.500
to find at least
one viable solution.

00:44:22.500 --> 00:44:24.700
And we started thinking
a little bit also

00:44:24.700 --> 00:44:26.880
like to do this in a chain
of thoughts kind of way,

00:44:26.880 --> 00:44:28.800
where you are doing
a chain of thought,

00:44:28.800 --> 00:44:31.580
but verifying through all the
steps into our chain of thoughts

00:44:31.580 --> 00:44:34.220
instead, and then pruning
from these branches

00:44:34.220 --> 00:44:37.780
that are violating anything.

00:44:37.780 --> 00:44:38.340
Yeah.

00:44:38.340 --> 00:44:41.940
So in terms of sequential,
you're saying that for easier

00:44:41.940 --> 00:44:45.200
ones, easier problems--
for harder problems,

00:44:45.200 --> 00:44:48.620
you mean you need more
exploration potentially

00:44:48.620 --> 00:44:49.660
for easier--

00:44:49.660 --> 00:44:51.740
To the correct answer.

00:44:51.740 --> 00:44:57.980
Or for easier problems,
because like any kind of path

00:44:57.980 --> 00:44:59.760
might lead us to
the correct answer,

00:44:59.760 --> 00:45:03.460
we might not need to explore
different parallel solutions.

00:45:03.460 --> 00:45:07.600
Yeah, that is an intuitive kind
of way of thinking about this.

00:45:10.900 --> 00:45:12.320
Any other thoughts?

00:45:19.060 --> 00:45:22.220
In general, this is a
very interesting kind

00:45:22.220 --> 00:45:23.760
of problem to think about.

00:45:23.760 --> 00:45:27.500
How do you-- let's assume that
these tokens that you generate

00:45:27.500 --> 00:45:30.760
is like a knob that you
have to increase problem,

00:45:30.760 --> 00:45:34.100
the quality of the responses,
how do you allocate this knob?

00:45:34.100 --> 00:45:38.700
And how do you elicit these
generations from the model?

00:45:38.700 --> 00:45:40.680
So let's think about
in the following,

00:45:40.680 --> 00:45:43.700
I'm going to talk about
one way to optimize

00:45:43.700 --> 00:45:47.830
these kind of test time scaling
to generate optimized answers.

00:45:47.830 --> 00:45:51.930
And that is the Archon paper,
an architecture search framework

00:45:51.930 --> 00:45:55.210
for inference time scaling.

00:45:55.210 --> 00:45:57.610
So the problem
state-- and your TA

00:45:57.610 --> 00:46:00.810
is one of the
co-authors of this work.

00:46:00.810 --> 00:46:03.650
So the problem in this
paper is that, again, we

00:46:03.650 --> 00:46:07.650
are interested to see
how we can mix and match

00:46:07.650 --> 00:46:10.050
different inference time
scaling methods to build

00:46:10.050 --> 00:46:14.890
the optimize the frontier
of capability or correctness

00:46:14.890 --> 00:46:18.530
versus cost.

00:46:18.530 --> 00:46:20.870
And the other thing that--

00:46:23.530 --> 00:46:26.650
again, all of these questions
basically saying the same thing

00:46:26.650 --> 00:46:29.630
that how we can optimize
assigning inference

00:46:29.630 --> 00:46:32.450
compute two different tasks.

00:46:32.450 --> 00:46:37.090
And basically, design mechanisms
that gets us to the high quality

00:46:37.090 --> 00:46:43.410
answers while not wasting too
many tokens and generations.

00:46:43.410 --> 00:46:46.830
So here is the arc--
like in this case,

00:46:46.830 --> 00:46:49.970
we thought of inference
scaling in this project

00:46:49.970 --> 00:46:56.170
as an inference
architecture design problem.

00:46:56.170 --> 00:47:00.430
So here is how the
Archon framework works.

00:47:00.430 --> 00:47:03.610
On the input side, we had
a set of target benchmarks

00:47:03.610 --> 00:47:06.630
that we wanted to get good
results on these benchmarks.

00:47:06.630 --> 00:47:09.210
So these are the benchmarks
we are optimizing for.

00:47:09.210 --> 00:47:11.930
There is an inference
called budget.

00:47:11.930 --> 00:47:14.730
And then there's also a
set of available LLMs,

00:47:14.730 --> 00:47:18.410
because we can always mix and
match different models also

00:47:18.410 --> 00:47:23.330
to get to a correct answer
for a input question.

00:47:23.330 --> 00:47:25.770
And then we have a set of
inference time techniques, which

00:47:25.770 --> 00:47:29.130
I'm going to describe
in the following slides.

00:47:29.130 --> 00:47:33.970
And then we designed
this optimizer.

00:47:33.970 --> 00:47:35.090
It's called itest.

00:47:35.090 --> 00:47:40.290
It's inference time architecture
search that basically tells us

00:47:40.290 --> 00:47:43.050
how to put together
these different models

00:47:43.050 --> 00:47:45.660
and different inference
time techniques together,

00:47:45.660 --> 00:47:49.100
whether it's parallel sampling,
sequential, and all that,

00:47:49.100 --> 00:47:53.360
in order to get
high-quality results given

00:47:53.360 --> 00:47:56.480
the budget, the inference
called budget that we have-- how

00:47:56.480 --> 00:48:00.180
to optimize the quality
of results based on that.

00:48:00.180 --> 00:48:02.280
And then the output
is this architecture

00:48:02.280 --> 00:48:04.680
that mixes and matches
these different techniques

00:48:04.680 --> 00:48:08.240
and models together.

00:48:08.240 --> 00:48:11.560
So let's take a look at
different inference time

00:48:11.560 --> 00:48:12.880
operations.

00:48:12.880 --> 00:48:16.180
So first, we have the general--
we call it the generation,

00:48:16.180 --> 00:48:24.120
but this is basically just
sampling from the model.

00:48:24.120 --> 00:48:28.460
And this can be part of the
repeated sampling approach.

00:48:28.460 --> 00:48:31.000
So if we generate n--

00:48:31.000 --> 00:48:33.560
we use n generations
at a time, that

00:48:33.560 --> 00:48:37.240
means we have generated n
different responses to our input

00:48:37.240 --> 00:48:38.100
question.

00:48:38.100 --> 00:48:41.560
So that's one type of
inference time scaling.

00:48:41.560 --> 00:48:45.600
Another one is fusion, which was
surprisingly a very effective

00:48:45.600 --> 00:48:46.440
method.

00:48:46.440 --> 00:48:53.200
So fusion is, let's say, we
have K different responses,

00:48:53.200 --> 00:48:56.080
and we want to generate
one output response out

00:48:56.080 --> 00:49:02.840
of these K. Fusion
takes an LLM and asks

00:49:02.840 --> 00:49:06.700
an LLM the same exact
question that I just said.

00:49:06.700 --> 00:49:09.500
Basically, it says,
here's the input question.

00:49:09.500 --> 00:49:13.760
Here are K different responses
to this input question.

00:49:13.760 --> 00:49:20.000
Given all of this, generate or
synthesize one output response

00:49:20.000 --> 00:49:21.580
for the input question.

00:49:21.580 --> 00:49:25.800
Basically, we are showing all of
the K generations to the model,

00:49:25.800 --> 00:49:30.840
such that the LLM can generate
an output answer while being

00:49:30.840 --> 00:49:38.680
aware of all the possible ways
that this question is answered.

00:49:38.680 --> 00:49:44.340
And this is-- it goes under the
category of sequential update.

00:49:44.340 --> 00:49:46.800
We have this parallel
samples, but then we

00:49:46.800 --> 00:49:49.560
are kind of fusing
them into one answer.

00:49:49.560 --> 00:49:56.040
Then there is the
critic, which basically,

00:49:56.040 --> 00:49:59.000
for any given response,
we can ask a model

00:49:59.000 --> 00:50:04.160
to generate or
describe the weaknesses

00:50:04.160 --> 00:50:06.680
or strengths of these response.

00:50:06.680 --> 00:50:12.280
And that could be one kind
of inference scaling action.

00:50:12.280 --> 00:50:14.000
And then we have the ranker.

00:50:14.000 --> 00:50:16.320
This is-- again, these
are all prompting based

00:50:16.320 --> 00:50:18.220
optimization for the model.

00:50:18.220 --> 00:50:21.960
We haven't trained the model
to do this specific test time

00:50:21.960 --> 00:50:22.820
scaling task.

00:50:22.820 --> 00:50:27.440
We just ask the model in
the instruction prompt that,

00:50:27.440 --> 00:50:29.920
can you rank these
generated responses

00:50:29.920 --> 00:50:34.660
from the previous step
based on their quality?

00:50:38.320 --> 00:50:41.390
And then the verifiers
generates the-- returns

00:50:41.390 --> 00:50:45.950
the response while also talk
about the reasoning of why

00:50:45.950 --> 00:50:52.630
it came to this kind of way
to score a certain answer.

00:50:52.630 --> 00:50:55.610
I'll get to the
next two unit tests

00:50:55.610 --> 00:50:57.370
generation and
evaluation in a bit.

00:50:57.370 --> 00:51:00.910
But just to show you how
effective these different

00:51:00.910 --> 00:51:03.510
inference time scaling
techniques are,

00:51:03.510 --> 00:51:13.890
let's take a look at this
win rate for what tasks--

00:51:13.890 --> 00:51:15.630
we don't have the task here.

00:51:15.630 --> 00:51:19.870
But it's one of the task
of one of these benchmarks

00:51:19.870 --> 00:51:23.330
that I don't know if
you remember the task.

00:51:23.330 --> 00:51:23.830
Yeah.

00:51:26.390 --> 00:51:31.190
Some question answering
reasoning benchmark.

00:51:31.190 --> 00:51:34.130
So here, let's take a look
at how these things work.

00:51:34.130 --> 00:51:36.830
So first, we have--

00:51:36.830 --> 00:51:41.430
let's say, we have a number of
repeated samples from the model.

00:51:41.430 --> 00:51:44.570
It goes from 1 to
10 on the left side.

00:51:44.570 --> 00:51:46.990
Let's focus on the
graph on the left side.

00:51:46.990 --> 00:51:51.390
And then the orange one is
the kind of worst possible way

00:51:51.390 --> 00:51:52.210
of approaching it.

00:51:52.210 --> 00:51:55.150
We just randomly
select one and look

00:51:55.150 --> 00:51:59.670
at the quality and the
correctness of this response.

00:51:59.670 --> 00:52:04.650
The green one is when we
use the model to rank them,

00:52:04.650 --> 00:52:07.870
to rank the responses, and
we select the top one ranked

00:52:07.870 --> 00:52:13.030
by the model and report that.

00:52:13.030 --> 00:52:16.950
That already does better
than the orange one.

00:52:16.950 --> 00:52:20.650
The blue one is
Oracle selection,

00:52:20.650 --> 00:52:23.350
meaning let's assume
we are not mixing

00:52:23.350 --> 00:52:26.190
these responses in any way.

00:52:26.190 --> 00:52:30.110
We just select the
best one generated.

00:52:30.110 --> 00:52:31.990
Let's say, we have
an Oracle verifier

00:52:31.990 --> 00:52:34.590
that can tell us which
one of these is correct.

00:52:34.590 --> 00:52:37.060
And that obviously
does better than just

00:52:37.060 --> 00:52:41.620
asking the model to rank them
because it's an Oracle verifier.

00:52:41.620 --> 00:52:44.100
And then we get to
the red one, which is

00:52:44.100 --> 00:52:46.700
where when we use the fusion.

00:52:46.700 --> 00:52:52.060
So basically, for each point
here, let's say, we are at 5,

00:52:52.060 --> 00:52:55.260
the red data point is basically
we have given these five

00:52:55.260 --> 00:52:59.060
responses to the model and
ask the model to synthesize

00:52:59.060 --> 00:53:03.480
one answer, one final answer,
given all this information.

00:53:03.480 --> 00:53:06.940
And it's so interesting, and
this paradigm is so powerful

00:53:06.940 --> 00:53:11.060
that that can on its own
improve the quality of responses

00:53:11.060 --> 00:53:15.260
over Oracle selection.

00:53:15.260 --> 00:53:20.460
And then finally, the
purple one is where we--

00:53:20.460 --> 00:53:27.320
instead of fusing
all the responses,

00:53:27.320 --> 00:53:33.300
we first selected the
top five, and then we

00:53:33.300 --> 00:53:34.840
went about fusing them.

00:53:34.840 --> 00:53:37.900
So it seems like some sort
of a filtering of the two

00:53:37.900 --> 00:53:41.220
does better.

00:53:41.220 --> 00:53:44.780
Now on the right side, we are
doing a different experiment,

00:53:44.780 --> 00:53:47.292
and that is we are--

00:53:47.292 --> 00:53:48.000
on the left side.

00:53:48.000 --> 00:53:50.720
We had only one model
generating everything.

00:53:50.720 --> 00:53:52.540
On the left side,
we had-- we were

00:53:52.540 --> 00:53:54.520
using-- we were adding models.

00:53:54.520 --> 00:53:57.860
So first one is just one
model generating one answer,

00:53:57.860 --> 00:54:00.540
two the baseline is two
different models generating

00:54:00.540 --> 00:54:03.520
one answer, and 10 is
10 different models,

00:54:03.520 --> 00:54:05.220
each generating one answer.

00:54:05.220 --> 00:54:09.300
And we see that, of
course, the random result

00:54:09.300 --> 00:54:12.980
becomes worse because the
model we looked on the left

00:54:12.980 --> 00:54:17.340
was I think it was among the
better models in this ensemble.

00:54:17.340 --> 00:54:22.540
But we still see the same
trend, even if these responses

00:54:22.540 --> 00:54:25.980
are coming from different
models, basically the same trend

00:54:25.980 --> 00:54:29.580
of how these all
follow each other.

00:54:29.580 --> 00:54:30.620
Yes.

00:54:30.620 --> 00:54:32.580
Which model is fusing?

00:54:32.580 --> 00:54:34.700
If you're using 10
different models,

00:54:34.700 --> 00:54:38.420
you're asking somebody
else to rank or fuse your--

00:54:38.420 --> 00:54:39.980
I think it's the
model number one

00:54:39.980 --> 00:54:41.820
in this case, the
same [INAUDIBLE] that

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:43.460
was in all of those.

00:54:43.460 --> 00:54:47.020
And this was the way we
created the ensemble was first

00:54:47.020 --> 00:54:49.620
with the best model and then
added the second best model

00:54:49.620 --> 00:54:50.240
and so on.

00:54:56.220 --> 00:54:58.780
Now let's take a
look at a couple

00:54:58.780 --> 00:55:01.440
other inference time techniques.

00:55:01.440 --> 00:55:04.600
And that is unit test
generation and evaluation.

00:55:04.600 --> 00:55:09.180
I think one of you was
mentioning something like this

00:55:09.180 --> 00:55:11.980
earlier as well, that
where we can ask the model

00:55:11.980 --> 00:55:14.340
to generate these
unit tests, say

00:55:14.340 --> 00:55:18.700
when it gets to coding task
or math, math problem-solving

00:55:18.700 --> 00:55:22.020
or other reasoning tasks.

00:55:22.020 --> 00:55:24.720
And the unit test
evaluation is even crazier.

00:55:24.720 --> 00:55:27.600
Like instead of
running the unit test,

00:55:27.600 --> 00:55:32.570
you're asking the model to
evaluate a generated answer

00:55:32.570 --> 00:55:36.410
against a unit test.

00:55:36.410 --> 00:55:40.570
For example, here
is a prompt check

00:55:40.570 --> 00:55:43.210
for-- so the coding
problem in this case

00:55:43.210 --> 00:55:47.410
is check for balanceness of
round brackets in an input

00:55:47.410 --> 00:55:47.910
string.

00:55:47.910 --> 00:55:49.630
That's a coding question.

00:55:49.630 --> 00:55:51.570
And then here is
an example input.

00:55:51.570 --> 00:55:56.770
Here is the type of unit test
that the model could generate.

00:55:56.770 --> 00:56:00.050
For example, given a string
with an odd number of brackets,

00:56:00.050 --> 00:56:03.050
the solution should output
no, which is like a good unit

00:56:03.050 --> 00:56:04.930
test in this case.

00:56:04.930 --> 00:56:08.110
Another one is when a closing
bracket is encountered,

00:56:08.110 --> 00:56:11.090
it must match the most
recently open brackets

00:56:11.090 --> 00:56:12.970
that hasn't been matched yet.

00:56:12.970 --> 00:56:15.725
That's another kind of
reasonable unit test.

00:56:15.725 --> 00:56:17.850
And then you can ask the
model to actually generate

00:56:17.850 --> 00:56:20.970
the code to test this as well.

00:56:20.970 --> 00:56:22.943
Now let's take a
look at the types.

00:56:22.943 --> 00:56:25.110
I don't know if you guys
see this well but the type.

00:56:25.110 --> 00:56:26.527
But this is so
big, there's no way

00:56:26.527 --> 00:56:30.190
for me to put it
here with large font.

00:56:30.190 --> 00:56:34.390
But this is the type of
architectures that Archon found.

00:56:34.390 --> 00:56:37.570
So basically now think
about it this way.

00:56:37.570 --> 00:56:40.010
We have different ways
of mixing and matching

00:56:40.010 --> 00:56:42.230
models and inference
time techniques,

00:56:42.230 --> 00:56:45.650
and we can define these
layers of optimization.

00:56:45.650 --> 00:56:48.990
For example, the first layer
in this case is generation.

00:56:48.990 --> 00:56:51.250
And then these generations
are from different models

00:56:51.250 --> 00:56:52.190
in this case.

00:56:52.190 --> 00:56:56.690
And then we have a
critic and a ranker.

00:56:56.690 --> 00:57:00.730
So basically, the critic will
criticize these generations

00:57:00.730 --> 00:57:04.650
and then we rank them and
then be the optimizer found

00:57:04.650 --> 00:57:07.730
that we could have a bunch
of users at this point,

00:57:07.730 --> 00:57:13.530
like each fuses these
responses into one output.

00:57:13.530 --> 00:57:19.110
And then we can go from there,
continue this critique ranker.

00:57:19.110 --> 00:57:21.930
And this was like a rather
complicated architecture.

00:57:21.930 --> 00:57:24.970
And the way that we
optimize this architecture

00:57:24.970 --> 00:57:28.800
is that we did some
kind of pre-processing

00:57:28.800 --> 00:57:32.020
to reduce the space
of optimization.

00:57:32.020 --> 00:57:33.920
For example, the
fact that we found

00:57:33.920 --> 00:57:38.480
this sequence of generation
critic ranker and user

00:57:38.480 --> 00:57:39.620
do really well.

00:57:39.620 --> 00:57:43.120
We did that offline, so we
limited the search space

00:57:43.120 --> 00:57:46.220
just because doing this
search is very expensive.

00:57:46.220 --> 00:57:48.300
We need to run a lot
of inference calls.

00:57:48.300 --> 00:57:52.000
But then the way we optimize it
that we had a held out data set

00:57:52.000 --> 00:57:56.320
that-- we had a training data
set that we could optimize

00:57:56.320 --> 00:57:59.920
against that and create
this accuracy versus

00:57:59.920 --> 00:58:03.800
the number of calls that we are
making to these models, kind

00:58:03.800 --> 00:58:07.100
of as a metric that we
are optimizing against.

00:58:07.100 --> 00:58:09.580
We want to maximize accuracy.

00:58:09.580 --> 00:58:16.560
And for any given budget,
inference budget that we have.

00:58:16.560 --> 00:58:19.380
And this is for some
of the coding problems.

00:58:19.380 --> 00:58:21.260
This was an optimized
architecture.

00:58:21.260 --> 00:58:24.360
Basically, we generate
a ton of samples.

00:58:24.360 --> 00:58:28.040
We generate unit tests
and then we evaluate them.

00:58:28.040 --> 00:58:33.760
Now, an interesting
property here

00:58:33.760 --> 00:58:38.440
is that we found that
stacking more and more layers

00:58:38.440 --> 00:58:40.060
of these inference
time techniques,

00:58:40.060 --> 00:58:42.560
or making it deep
in a way, actually

00:58:42.560 --> 00:58:49.000
does help with our accuracy.

00:58:49.000 --> 00:58:53.360
In this case, for example,
the right most pink graph

00:58:53.360 --> 00:58:57.400
is where we have an
ensemble, meaning we have

00:58:57.400 --> 00:58:59.140
different models in our loop.

00:58:59.140 --> 00:59:02.920
And we have three layers
of critiques and fusers

00:59:02.920 --> 00:59:06.080
and a final fuser layer.

00:59:06.080 --> 00:59:08.640
And this is significantly
better across many

00:59:08.640 --> 00:59:12.360
of these tasks over
both just using

00:59:12.360 --> 00:59:16.140
a simple the best
model only once,

00:59:16.140 --> 00:59:19.740
or the best model eight times
with one layer of fusion.

00:59:19.740 --> 00:59:22.160
So it seems like these
additional layers

00:59:22.160 --> 00:59:23.800
that we are adding
here is actually--

00:59:23.800 --> 00:59:26.040
just like in deep
learning, we are

00:59:26.040 --> 00:59:28.780
adding layers in pre-training
and the model gets better.

00:59:28.780 --> 00:59:31.000
Seems like these careful
kind of additions

00:59:31.000 --> 00:59:33.560
of these inference layers
are helping the model

00:59:33.560 --> 00:59:35.200
become more and more accurate.

00:59:35.200 --> 00:59:40.560
Across many of the
tasks this is true.

00:59:40.560 --> 00:59:43.960
And so now the
optimizer itself, we

00:59:43.960 --> 00:59:48.580
used an Bayesian optimizer again
to simplify the search space.

00:59:48.580 --> 00:59:50.880
We did a few things.

00:59:50.880 --> 00:59:53.200
For example, we
limited the optimizer

00:59:53.200 --> 00:59:57.520
to only use one inference
time technique per layer,

00:59:57.520 --> 01:00:00.760
and the first layer was
always just the generator.

01:00:00.760 --> 01:00:06.360
And then we had this cascade
of ranker, critic, verifier.

01:00:06.360 --> 01:00:09.000
They could go
anywhere, but we made

01:00:09.000 --> 01:00:12.340
certain kind of modifications
to the search space.

01:00:12.340 --> 01:00:16.600
For example, a critic should
go before a ranker or a fuser

01:00:16.600 --> 01:00:20.080
always because we did some
testing locally and saw

01:00:20.080 --> 01:00:22.480
this is a better choice.

01:00:22.480 --> 01:00:24.710
And then a unit
test generator must

01:00:24.710 --> 01:00:27.870
be followed by an evaluator up.

01:00:27.870 --> 01:00:32.750
And again, we use a Bayesian
optimizer to do this.

01:00:32.750 --> 01:00:39.150
This is often a very
powerful methodology,

01:00:39.150 --> 01:00:41.910
a ton of good open
source software

01:00:41.910 --> 01:00:43.690
to do Bayesian
optimization for you.

01:00:43.690 --> 01:00:45.630
Basically, you have
this search space

01:00:45.630 --> 01:00:50.715
that you want to configure it
with different kind of choices

01:00:50.715 --> 01:00:51.590
for the architecture.

01:00:51.590 --> 01:00:53.390
And the output side,
you have the accuracy

01:00:53.390 --> 01:00:55.710
and you want to
optimize for that.

01:00:55.710 --> 01:00:59.450
And this does better than greedy
search or random selection.

01:00:59.450 --> 01:01:01.350
We are much more sample
efficient in terms

01:01:01.350 --> 01:01:04.990
of number of configurations
that Archon searches

01:01:04.990 --> 01:01:09.550
until it reaches a final
kind of architecture,

01:01:09.550 --> 01:01:12.450
and we can define different
objectives for it.

01:01:12.450 --> 01:01:16.610
For example, available
models, inference time,

01:01:16.610 --> 01:01:22.110
inference, core budget is
one of them or some of them.

01:01:22.110 --> 01:01:26.150
The thing that I want you to
pay attention to in this graph

01:01:26.150 --> 01:01:29.550
is that this interesting
property that Archon

01:01:29.550 --> 01:01:32.370
is, at the end, generating
one response at the very end.

01:01:32.370 --> 01:01:36.670
So it's like you're optimizing
paths at one at the very end.

01:01:36.670 --> 01:01:42.350
And what was interesting
here is that while using just

01:01:42.350 --> 01:01:48.390
the open source models,
we could match or exceed

01:01:48.390 --> 01:01:51.270
these closed source
models at the time,

01:01:51.270 --> 01:01:53.790
the frontier closed
source model at the time,

01:01:53.790 --> 01:01:58.550
by a large margin across
many of these tasks in terms

01:01:58.550 --> 01:01:59.570
of paths at one.

01:01:59.570 --> 01:02:02.070
Here's the average path
at one improvement.

01:02:02.070 --> 01:02:04.830
And not only that,
we could train

01:02:04.830 --> 01:02:06.970
Archon to be task specific.

01:02:06.970 --> 01:02:09.450
So when we are doing the
Bayesian optimization,

01:02:09.450 --> 01:02:14.270
we only look into a certain
task and we test on that,

01:02:14.270 --> 01:02:17.210
or we could train Archon
to be general purpose.

01:02:17.210 --> 01:02:21.140
So we wanted it to do
better good across many--

01:02:21.140 --> 01:02:23.120
to do well across many tasks.

01:02:23.120 --> 01:02:26.480
And even that the general
purpose one does really,

01:02:26.480 --> 01:02:29.220
really well and better
than the frontier

01:02:29.220 --> 01:02:31.560
models across these tasks.

01:02:31.560 --> 01:02:35.500
And that was a very
interesting observation

01:02:35.500 --> 01:02:38.420
that you can design these
inference time architectures

01:02:38.420 --> 01:02:42.900
that do well beyond the
task or the limited tasks

01:02:42.900 --> 01:02:44.280
that they're trained on.

01:02:44.280 --> 01:02:48.380
So on average, in this case,
we were outperforming GPT-4.0

01:02:48.380 --> 01:02:54.740
or Claude 3.5 Sonnet in passage
one by an average of 14.1%

01:02:54.740 --> 01:02:58.940
across these instruction
following reasoning and math

01:02:58.940 --> 01:03:00.820
encoding problems.

01:03:00.820 --> 01:03:05.600
Here are some questions, that
I want you to think about.

01:03:05.600 --> 01:03:07.780
But since we're out of time.

01:03:07.780 --> 01:03:11.020
Hopefully, you think
about it yourself.

01:03:11.020 --> 01:03:15.730
And hope you have
a happy weekend.

